# Line endings.
#
# This repository is written on Windows and executed under WSL against the same
# files on /mnt/c. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true, so without this
# file a fresh clone hands the scripts back with CRLF endings and every one of
# them fails as `/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory` -- on the
# machine they were written on. Store and check out LF everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.pdf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.img   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.squashfs binary

# Bench transcripts are evidence, not source.
#
# The device sends CR in its own output -- the loader's PHY format strings end
# \r\n -- so the `* text=auto eol=lf` rule above, which exists so a script
# edited on Windows still runs under WSL, would quietly rewrite what the board
# actually said.  Measured 2026-08-23: it would have taken 20 bytes out of
# B.log and 47 out of E.log.  A transcript that has been normalised is not a
# transcript.
bench/**        -text

# And qemu captures are transcripts for the same reason, 2026-08-26.
#
# `report.c` ends every line `\r\n`, so a serial capture out of the emulator
# carries CR exactly as a bench log does. Measured on the first one committed:
# `git add` warned `CRLF will be replaced by LF` on
# `qemu/2026-08-26/probe3.txt` -- 125 lines, so 125 bytes of what the payload
# actually put on the wire. The `.build` file beside it records the capture's
# own sha256, and a normalised capture does not match it.
qemu/**         -text
